Abstract
Use masked autoregressive flows (MAFs) and kernel density estimators (KDEs) to learn marginal (nuisance-free) posterior densities of science parameters
Applications across astrophysics

1 Introduction
Bayesian analysis uses Bayes theorem to update probability of a given model (posterior), using existing knowledge (prior) and probability distribution of the data (likelihood)
Nuisance parameters characterise foreground/instrumental effects, leading to high dimensional parameter spaces when only a few are of interest
Margarine calculates nuisance-marginalised posteriors through MAFs and KDEs
Density estimators model a probability distribution from a set of samples
Kullback-Leibler divergence determines how much information has been gained when moving from prior to posterior - marginal KL divergence is gain in information of core science parameters - measure of contraction between the two distributions
Bayesian model dimensionality (BMD) is number of parameters constrained during inference - marginal BMD used to compare number of core science parameters

2 Theory
Nested sampling algorithm aims to estimate evidence Z: use prior to generate samples of parameters Θ, calculate likelihood, then approximate the integral over Θ
Evidence can be used to compare models - higher evidence suggests preference for a particular model
Marginal posteriors for core science parameters found by integrating posterior over nuisance parameters
Difficult to calculate the high dimensional integral over nuisance parameters - use density estimators to generate samples from marginalised posterior distribution
Nuisance-free likelihood = marginal posterior * Z / marginal prior
Marginal posterior and prior found from samples using a DE
Marginal KL divergence corresponds to astrophysical parameters - approximately equal to log(prior volume/posterior volume) - posterior must be normalised
BMD gives measure of effective number of parameters being constrained - can quantify tensions between different experimental results

3 Density estimators in practice
Normalising flows transform a simple normal distribution into a complex one
Autoregressive means each parameter is only conditional on the previous ones
In a masked autoregressive flow, some connections between the input and output are masked out to respect the dependence of the variables
Kernel density estimation: place a small (Gaussian) kernel at every sample data point, then add individual distributions to produce an estimate of overall probability density

4 Applications of Margarine
Train density estimators on posteriors from individual fits for core science parameters from two different experiments to generate nuisance free likelihood functions
Then log(L_AB) = log(L_A) + log(L_B)
Emulators are a computationally cheap approximation of complex models - can be used to make priors

5 Conclusions
Evaluation of marginal KL divergences and BMDs let you compare different experiments targeting the same signals with different nuisance parameters
MAFs perform better than KDEs - more accurate and scale better with sample size and dimension
Nuisance-free likelihood lets you efficiently combine constraints of different data sets
Margarine can create priors using experimental data/simulations
